  "enhanced_information": "The patient presents with symptoms consistent with hyperthyroidism, including nausea, vomiting, palpitations, and diarrhea, alongside a heart rate of 145/min. The laboratory results show a low TSH level (0.28 mIU/L) and an elevated Total T4 level (12 µg/dL), which supports the diagnosis of hyperthyroidism. To further assess the patient's thyroid hormone status, measuring Free T4 levels is crucial, as it provides a more accurate reflection of the active thyroid hormone available in the body compared to Total T4. While Total T3 levels could provide additional information, Free T4 is more directly related to the patient's symptoms and is the preferred test in this context. Other tests, such as TPO antibodies or TRAb, are not immediately necessary for diagnosing hyperthyroidism in this case. Therefore, the most useful additional laboratory test is Free thyroxine (T4) levels.",
